---
Render a Markdown document, or show an image, in the terminal

Usage: kiri [OPTIONS] [FILE]...

Arguments:
  [FILE]...
          Markdown, text, CSV or TSV, an image, a Mermaid diagram, or a directory
          
          A Markdown file is rendered, a CSV or TSV file shown as a table, an image or a Mermaid
          diagram (.mmd) shown on its own, any other text file shown as it is, highlighted when kiri
          knows its language, and a directory shows its README. Several files are shown one after
          another, each under a header that names it; one that cannot be shown is reported, and the
          others are still shown. Without one, or with -, kiri reads standard input unless it is a
          terminal; - can be given only once.

Options:
      --doctor
          Show what kiri finds out about the terminal, for a bug report
          
          The report also tells which settings kiri uses. It is Markdown when written to a file.

      --list-emojis
          List the emoji shortcodes, such as :smile:
          
          A terminal shows them as a table; a pipe or a file gets one line per emoji.

      --completions <SHELL>
          Write the shell completion script to standard output
          
          Install it for bash with eval "$(kiri --completions bash)" in ~/.bashrc, or save it as a
          file in the shell's completion directory.
          
          [possible values: bash, zsh, fish, elvish, powershell, nushell]

      --raw
          With --doctor: the report as Markdown, also in a terminal
          
          The report then leaves out its sample, so it can be copied into a bug report.

      --format <FORMAT>
          What the file is, such as markdown, csv or png
          
          The formats are markdown, csv, tsv, mermaid (a diagram shown on its own), text (the file
          as it is, without rendering it), image, or an image format such as svg, png or jpg. By
          default its extension and content tell.

      --width <WIDTH>
          Width in cells; defaults to the terminal width

      --mode <MODE>
          Which terminal features to use [default: auto]
          
          Colours stay off when NO_COLOR is set.

          Possible values:
          - auto:  Ask the terminal; plain text in a pipe
          - plain: Text only, no escape sequences
          - ansi:  Colours and attributes, no kitty extensions
          - kitty: All kitty features, without asking the terminal
          - sixel: Sixel images, whether or not the terminal lists sixel

      --theme <THEME>
          Theme file, or a theme's name
          
          A name, such as dark, is NAME.toml in the themes directory, or the built-in light or dark
          paper. It overrides KIRI_THEME and the configuration file.

      --light
          A light page in greys, as --theme light
          
          Whatever the terminal's colours. Code gets colours for a light background.

      --dark
          A dark page in greys, as --theme dark
          
          Whatever the terminal's colours. Code gets colours for a dark background.

  -M, --no-margins
          No margins, indents or padding, for copying text
          
          Leaves out the margins, heading indents and code block padding.

      --margins
          Keep the margins, whatever KIRI_MARGINS and the configuration say

  -I, --no-images
          Show images as their alt text and diagrams as code
          
          Images are not read. An image or diagram file given on its own is still shown.

      --images
          Show images, whatever KIRI_NO_IMAGES and the configuration say
          
          Diagrams are shown too, unless they are off.

      --paging <WHEN>
          When to show the document in the pager
          
          With auto, the pager opens when the output is a terminal and all the files together,
          images and diagrams too, are longer than the window. Standard input and --doctor are paged
          only with always.
          
          [possible values: always, auto, never]

  -p
          Show the document in the pager: --paging=always

  -P
          Print the document without the pager: --paging=never

      --follow <WHEN>
          Follow a lone file in the pager
          
          With on, kiri reads the file again whenever it changes, with the view kept where it is;
          with auto it also moves the view to a change; off unless set. Following pages the file,
          however short, unless paging is never. F goes from off to on to auto in the pager.
          
          [possible values: on, off, auto]

  -f
          Follow the file, in the pager: --follow=on

      --fa
          Follow the file and move to a change: --follow=auto

  -F
          Do not follow the file: --follow=off

      --follow-jump <WHICH>
          Which change auto-follow moves the view to
          
          When a reload changed the document in more than one place: first, last or most (the one
          that takes the most rows); first unless set.
          
          [possible values: first, last, most]

      --initial-load <HOW>
          Whether the pager waits for images before it shows the document
          
          With wait, the document is shown once its images and diagrams have loaded; with
          background, at once, with each image as it comes; wait unless set. Only where the pager
          surely opens: with -p or -f, or with paging on auto for a document longer than the window,
          each image counted at three rows; otherwise kiri waits.
          
          [possible values: wait, background]

      --passthrough <HOW>
          Whether kitty's images pass through tmux
          
          With detect, kiri wraps them for tmux when it runs inside tmux and turns on the pane's
          allow-passthrough, as `kitten icat` does; with tmux, it wraps them always; with none,
          never; detect unless set.
          
          [possible values: detect, none, tmux]

      --front-matter <STYLE>
          How to show the front matter
          
          Front matter is the metadata block at the start of a Markdown file. The styles are table,
          code (a code block), hide, or off (read as Markdown); table unless set.
          
          [possible values: table, code, hide, off]

      --headings <HOW>
          How headings show
          
          auto draws them large with text sizing, or as images where the terminal has no text
          sizing; sized only with text sizing; images always where images can be drawn; plain in one
          row; auto unless set.
          
          [possible values: auto, sized, images, plain]

      --table-rows <STYLE>
          Table row style
          
          The styles are zebra (every second row shaded where the terminal's colours are known),
          lines, indent (the lines after the first of a wrapped value start two cells in), underline
          (each row's last line underlined), or off; zebra unless set.
          
          [possible values: off, zebra, lines, indent, underline]

      --diagrams <SWITCH>
          Whether to draw diagrams and formulas
          
          Diagrams in code blocks, such as Mermaid, and formulas: on (drawn as images) or off (shown
          as code); on unless set.
          
          [possible values: on, off]

      --diagram-timeout <SECS>
          How long a program may take to draw one diagram, in seconds

      --diagram-panel <SWITCH>
          Whether a diagram in the other page's colours lies on a panel
          
          on or off; on unless set.
          
          [possible values: on, off]

      --render-errors <WHERE>
          Where to tell why a diagram could not be drawn
          
          The places are inline (in the document, below its code), stderr (a warning) or both; both
          unless set.
          
          [possible values: inline, stderr, both]

      --no-mouse
          Leave the mouse to the terminal in the pager
          
          The pager takes the mouse unless set, so that the wheel scrolls it, also inside tmux.
          While it has the mouse, kitty selects text with Shift and the mouse.

      --mouse
          Let the pager take the mouse
          
          Whatever KIRI_MOUSE and the configuration say.

      --reading
          Experimental: the text in a proportional font, as a browser shows it
          
          Headings, paragraphs, lists and quotes are drawn as images, so their text cannot be
          selected or copied. It needs a terminal that draws images, such as kitty; elsewhere the
          document shows as text. The option's name may still change. On unless turned off;
          KIRI_READING and the configuration file set it too, and --no-reading turns it off.

      --no-reading
          Reading mode off, whatever KIRI_READING and the configuration say

      --wide <HOW>
          Experimental: wide code and tables wrap or scroll in reading mode
          
          With wrap, a code block or table wider than reading mode's area wraps to fit it; with
          scroll, it keeps its lines and words whole and scrolls sideways in the pager, where W
          switches between the two. Printing always wraps. wrap unless set; KIRI_WIDE and the
          configuration file set it too. The option's name may still change.
          
          [possible values: wrap, scroll]

      --max-image-size <MB>
          Largest image file to read, in megabytes [default: 10]
          
          At most 100; larger images show their alt text.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Images and diagrams are drawn only in terminals that support kitty's graphics protocol with Unicode
placeholders, such as kitty 0.28 or later and Ghostty 1.0 or later; elsewhere images show their alt
text and diagrams their code.
